**Short Story:**  
Gnommik Twinkletoes cackled as his tiny Succubus, Sizzlepuff, danced around a jungle troll, her purple glow hypnotizing it into dropping a voodoo mask. "See? Trolls love shiny things!" he chirped, pocketing the loot. But when a pack of raptors ambushed, Sizzlepuff’s *Seduction* flickered—delaying just long enough for one to claw Gnommik’s ankle. "Next time, I’ll respec!" he grumbled, eyeing the Voidwalker he’d dismissed before. "At least *he* doesn’t get distracted by shiny rocks mid-combat."

---

**Answer:**  
The Succubus’s **Seduction** ability (which charms enemies) often *accidentally* charms the warlock’s *own* target or nearby allies during combat, causing frustration and failure during questing and leveling. This makes Voidwalkers—immune to crowd control and stable—seem more reliable, even if Succubus is stronger *when it works*. Thus, most warlocks stick with Voidwalkers for consistency, despite the Succubus’s potential. [end of text]


